<p>President Trump said the European Union must fulfill or approve its side of a trade framework with the United States by July 4 or face “much higher” U.S. tariffs, after a call with European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen on Thursday. Trump wrote on Truth Social that he had been “waiting patiently” for the EU to cut its tariffs to zero under the agreement reached last year in Turnberry, Scotland. The deadline followed Trump’s earlier threat to raise U.S. tariffs on EU cars and trucks to 25% from 15% this week. Several accounts described the announcement as an extension of that threat. The trade framework struck last July set a 15% U.S. tariff on most EU goods, while the EU was expected to cut tariffs on U.S. industrial goods and provide duty-free quotas for some American agricultural and seafood products. Von der Leyen said on X that both sides remained committed to implementing the agreement.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/CGKGQXn5Roo8GS23nawkhKwxkfqxVahe9PAFsR_ImOIpY79TpGXyItADTxF8rOq1k61jeNR1qLh2Q1OaZ9qNVkkjcqyIQz11d1z-gsXTk3oDozGPdWHuVDc1MmCZspklWXjmrrBQDSOyVves82gf-I0vE3bDxAJmO8C8ZyZkdwi3ifU1MbBJrbb51KfBGhsfJHjihvbOkxaNEeOhJiTHOVmzyJifQEoYvPAN9jvMzh3IA4a1clQ-k3c1bEyidmtXrUy3sPjJOuJjjfVsiA' alt='U.S. President Donald Trump shakes hands with European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen in Turnberry, Scotland, on July 27, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Legal backdrop: A U.S. Supreme Court ruling in February found Trump exceeded his authority in imposing a broad range of tariffs, after which the administration used a temporary 10% duty and pursued other legal routes.</li><li>EU process: The European Parliament conditionally approved the deal in March, but its final form still needs agreement with EU member states through the Council of the European Union.</li><li>Scope ambiguity: Independent.com.mt reported it was unclear whether Trump meant higher rates would apply to all EU goods or to a narrower set of products.</li></ul><b